<h2>The seizure at Terminal-3</h2>
<p>Acting on prior information, officers of the Narcotics Control Bureau intercepted two women arriving from Bangkok on 22 May 2026. From the petitioner's backpack, 986 grams of marijuana were recovered; from her companion's, 990 grams — a combined 1.976 kilograms, squarely within the intermediate band for ganja. Both were arrested, and the petitioner remained in custody from the date of seizure. The case, Crime No. VIII/19/DZU/2026, proceeded before the Special Judge (NDPS) at the Rouse Avenue Courts as SC 277/2026, with charges under Sections 8(C), 20(b) and 29 of the NDPS Act.</p>
<p>The petitioner's account was that she had been deceived into travelling abroad on the promise of employment and persuaded to carry a bag handed over by a third party. The NCB countered that the two accused acted in concert and pointed to a prior similar episode. The trial court refused bail in June 2026, when the investigation was young; by late September it had granted bail to the co-accused.</p>

<h2>The reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Quantity below the commercial line.</strong> The recovery being intermediate, Section 37's twin conditions did not govern, and ordinary bail principles applied.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation complete.</strong> The chargesheet stood filed; ten witnesses remained for trial. Custody could no longer be justified by investigative necessity.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Changed circumstances.</strong> The June rejection rested on the early stage of investigation — a ground that had disappeared, making the fresh application maintainable and meritorious.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Parity.</strong> The co-accused, with a nearly identical recovery, had bail from the trial court since 25 September 2026.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The court recorded that its observations were solely for deciding the bail application and would not influence the trial. Bail followed on a bond of Rs. 50,000 with one surety and the usual battery of conditions, including weekly reporting and witness non-contact.</p>
</div>

<h2>The quiet importance of quantity classification</h2>
<p>NDPS bail outcomes often turn less on advocacy than on arithmetic. The rigours of Section 37 — reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty, and unlikely to offend on bail — attach only to commercial quantities, besides certain specified offences. For ganja, the commercial threshold is 20 kilograms; a seizure of under two kilograms, though it sounds substantial, sits in the intermediate band where the usual considerations of custody period, stage of proceedings and antecedents decide the application. Counsel on both sides must therefore begin with the notification table: the classification fixes the legal regime before a single fact is argued.</p>
<p>The order also illustrates the practical meaning of a changed circumstance. A bail rejection is never res judicata; when the specific ground of rejection — here, a nascent investigation — ceases to exist, a renewed application is not a second bite but a fresh case. Courts respond to that framing far better than to a re-argument of what was already rejected.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Verify the substance, its purity where relevant, and the notified thresholds before forming any view on bail strategy in an NDPS matter.</p>
<p>Track the procedural milestones — filing of the chargesheet, framing of charge, examination of witnesses — and move afresh when a milestone dissolves the ground of an earlier rejection.</p>
<p>Alleged couriers should place their version — deception, employment promises, third-party bags — on record early and consistently; it frames the conspiracy allegation at trial.</p>
<p>Expect and prepare for reporting conditions; a realistic compliance plan (local address, working phone) makes the difference between an order that works and one that invites cancellation.</p>
</div>
